From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:05:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] mkfs: do not use `true` as a label

`match()` spells its success label `true:` and jumps to it with
`goto true;`. That was unambiguous in C99, where `true` was only ever a
macro from `<stdbool.h>` -- which this file does not include -- but C23
promotes `true` to a keyword, and a keyword cannot be a label:

    mkfs.c:3753:30: error: expected identifier or `*` before `true`
     3753 |                 goto true;
    mkfs.c:3760:5: error: expected `;` before `:` token
     3760 | true:

Rename it to `matched:`, which also says what the label means rather
than what it returns. No functional change, and nothing outside
`match()` can see it.

This is not a foreign-host concern -- the same collision will arrive on
illumos itself the moment the gate moves to C23 -- so it belongs in the
tool rather than in any compat shim.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Claude Opus 5)
---
 usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c b/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
index 292bf1ac06..b8acb5fd51 100644
--- a/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
+++ b/usr/src/cmd/fs.d/ufs/mkfs/mkfs.c
@@ -3750,14 +3750,14 @@ match(char *s)
 	cs = string;
 	while (*cs++ == *s) {
 		if (*s++ == '\0') {
-			goto true;
+			goto matched;
 		}
 	}
 	if (*s != '\0') {
 		return (0);
 	}
 
-true:
+matched:
 	cs--;
 	string = cs;
 	return (1);
